------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Heuristic uniqueness constraints for non-IdentifiedObject classes
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The following UNIQUE constraints are emitted for CIM classes that do not
-- inherit from IdentifiedObject and therefore carry a surrogate "id" PK rather
-- than a natural "mRID" PK. Because the profile catalog XML does not encode
-- which subset of columns forms the true natural composite key (that information
-- exists only in the IEC specification prose), these constraints span all
-- required (NOT NULL) non-surrogate columns as a heuristic guard. The
-- constraint may be wider than the true natural key but is never incorrect —
-- a wider UNIQUE prevents valid duplicate insertion; a missing UNIQUE permits
-- silent semantic corruption.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Cascade delete foreign key constraint definitions (for Compounds)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The following cascade delete constraints implement the compound value object
-- ownership model described in the file header. Each constraint takes the form:
-- 
-- ALTER TABLE <CompoundType> ADD CONSTRAINT ... FOREIGN KEY ("id")
-- REFERENCES <ParentTable> (<compoundColumn>) ON DELETE CASCADE;
-- 
-- This reverse-reference pattern means: when a parent row is deleted, any
-- compound child row whose "id" matches the parent's compound column value
-- is automatically deleted. Because each compound row has exactly one owner
-- (enforced by the unique UUID insertion directive above), this cascade
-- correctly and completely cleans up all compound child rows when their
-- parent is deleted — no application-level programmatic deletion is required.
-- 
-- Known DBMS constraint engine limitation: the DBMS enforces each cascade FK
-- constraint globally across all rows in the compound table. It has no awareness
-- of the per-row ownership convention. Consumers who violate the unique UUID
-- insertion directive — for example by reusing a compound row UUID in more than
-- one parent column — will encounter FK constraint violations at insert time,
-- since the DBMS will check the reused UUID against every cascade constraint
-- on the compound table simultaneously. This is by design: the constraint
-- violation serves as the enforcement mechanism for the ownership model.

